  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "We can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following product: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac 13 \\times 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we represent $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ using a model:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129500/assets/f08054646558ab944940f1f8f0d8a1077748f9882039e775c6cbdd4d307db102.png)\n\n \n\nMultiplication by a whole number is simply a repeated addition. And, since we need to multiply $\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}$ by ${\\color{blue}2},$ we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129500/assets/31fcef6d631262e49fc9c9297fd006f935fd7ee8182b0074d77ae22b626efbbc.png)\n\n \n\nThere are ${\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 2$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$",
